d-only as\n`artifacts/codexPL/granite-inspected.json`; it can replace the hand fixture in\nthat command. The checked-in example report uses the real inspection.\n`--only-input-representation` disables discovery of the other available Granite\nGGUFs. Otherwise the six known sibling filenames are considered only if present\nin the input model's directory and the input agrees with the calibrated Granite\narchitecture, dimensions, filename and weight byte counts. Missing quantizations\nare never invented. All other inputs enumerate their supplied representation.\n\nAdditional controls: `--budget-host-mib`, `--max-kld`,\n`--objective decode|ttft|balanced`, `--allow-extrapolation`, and\n`--resource-sample sample.json`. `--prompt-tokens` changes the analytical warm\nTTFT proxy and requires extrapolation for values other than 62; lab emission\nthen refuses because the actual mounted chat fixture contains 62 tokens.\nOutput JSON includes ranked feasible plans, every rejected candidate and its\nreason, fit coefficients/residuals, effective budgets and limitations. Invalid\ninputs exit 2; a valid budget with no feasible supported plan returns an empty\nlist and explicit rejections. An empty list does not prove no engine can run.\n`--emit-lab-tests` deliberately accepts only destinations under the two allowed\nwrite roots, including when symbolic links are involved.\n\n## Input adapter\n\nThe API is `plan(stats, host, budget, allow_extrapolation=False, top=5, ...)`.\nBudget keys are `gpu_mib`, optional `host_mib`, `ctx`, optional\n`max_quality_loss` (mean KLD), and `objective`.\n\n`adapt_stats()` accepts the T-MI producer's `model-stats/0` directly:\n`arch`, `n_layer`, `n_expert`, `n_expert_used`, `context_length`, `gguf_path`,\n`tensors[{role,bytes,...}]`, and\n`kv_cache.cache_types.TYPE.bytes_per_token` (total K plus V).\nShared FFN and unknown roles count toward nonexpert residency, never disappear.\nThe small hand fixture alternatively supplies `model_path`, `representation`,\n`expert_bytes`, `nonexpert_bytes`, and `kv_bytes_per_token` directly. A\n`totals.by_role` or `totals_by_role` byte mapping is another supported adapter.\nUnavailable KV byte counts are omitted; unavailable f16 KV is an error.\nUnknown models have no inferred quality measurement and require extrapolation.\nThe adapter checks shape/byte compatibility, not full GGUF content identity;\nqualified measurement must bind model and image SHA256 values.\n\nThe optional `resource-sample/0` adapter consumes successful, correctly unitized\n`gpu.memory.total`, `gpu.memory.used`, and `host.mem.available` fields. It limits\navailable budgets to device free MiB and available host bytes. Unavailable/error\nfields remain unknown and generate warnings; they never become zero. This is\nonly an availability hint: samples are non-atomic, can be stale, and do not\nreserve memory. Device totals are never relabeled as process allocations or\nadded to process/cgroup counters. Host RAM and GPU VRAM are separate budgets on\nthis discrete host; applying these fits to unified memory requires the explicit\nextrapolation flag and does not establish unified-memory accounting correctness.\n\n## Evidence and decision rules\n\n* **Expert precision before placement.** [D013](../../decisions/D013-mixed-precision-frontier.md)\n  identifies experts as about 90.4% of weight bytes. Consider available expert\n  representations before spending transfer or CPU-compute costs. mixE4K mean\n  KLD 0.040017 is substantially below uniform Q4_K_M 0.077526 at similar GPU\n  footprints. Q8_0 is 0.003389, mixE3K 0.135039, and mixE2K 0.771232.\n  These are representation changes, with measured drift on wikitext, not\n  computation-preserving improvements. [D009](../../decisions/D009-representation-screen.md),\n  [D010](../../decisions/D010-kl-quality-check.md) and D013 supply the numbers.\n* **Never recommend engine LRU below 60% resident-byte coverage on this discrete\n  PCIe host.** This is a conservative planner policy, **not a measured universal\n  60% breakpoint** and not an assumption that byte coverage equals routing hit\n  rate. [D022](../../decisions/D022-static-vs-moecache-matched.md) shows static\n  placement winning at every matched footprint, including c384 (~59% of the\n  648 MiB expert banks) and c640 (~99%).\n  [D019](../../decisions/D019-hybrid-harness-result.md) measures pinned H2D\n  admission at 37.9 us/expert versus 30.6 us CPU compute plus ~15 us per layer.\n  c640 remains an enumerated control but loses to static in the fitted ranking.\n* **Declare context.** [D023](../../decisions/D023-novmm-engine-under-budget.md)\n  measures 192 MiB f16 KV at ctx4096, 108 MiB compute and 778.7 MiB weights.\n  The model's default/max ctx131072 requires 6144 MiB f16 KV analytically;\n  KV then exceeds compressed weights. Context is a required constraint; the\n  planner never silently shortens it to fit. KV q8_0 and q4_0 use the inspector's\n  exact block accounting (102 and 54 MiB at ctx4096 here), require `-fa on`,\n  and remain uncalibrated for speed and combined quality.\n* **Auto-fit is not a baseline.** D023's virtual 900 MiB budget caused auto-fit\n  to place only 155 MiB on GPU and decode at 86 t/s, below explicit static n24.\n  Every emitted plan sets `-ngl 99 --fit off` and declares placement explicitly.\n  This also avoids the hybrid graph-before-weight-load failure in\n  [D027](../../decisions/D027-hybrid-split-protocol.md).\n* **Verify mechanism engagement before timing.**\n  [D024](../../decisions/D024-static-hot-rejected.md) rejected static-hot because\n  its allocated banks were never used. Every candidate gets a short engagement\n  test first. Require counters/backend logs or a qualified fingerprint; successful\n  process exit alone is insufficient. Greedy-output identity does not bound KLD.\n* **Do not predict normal RAM speed below the working set.**\n  [D015](../../decisions/D015-ssd-tier-emulation.md) demonstrates a page-cache\n  cliff and severe read amplification. The planner requires a conservative\n  complete file-backed weight set plus 400 MiB anonymous allowance. It rejects\n  tighter host budgets rather than interpolating the contention-affected SSD\n  timing. D022 warm cgroup peaks (~412–418 MiB) do not include every residency\n  scenario or all preexisting page-cache charges and cannot certify this budget.\n* **Do not scale throughput inversely with bytes.**\n  [D011](../../decisions/D011-bandwidth-and-q8-profile.md) and D009 show Q8_0\n  improving clean decode by ~32.5% over BF16, but Q4_K_M is slower than Q8_0.\n  Kernel/dequantization and launch costs matter. Representation transfers use\n  measured screen endpoints when available and are explicitly extrapolated.\n\n## Fits and calibration boundaries\n\n`calibration.json` preserves all 27 D022 samples from\n`lab/jobs/job-ad883810b3/att-1/result.json`, with decision, job, attempt and test\nIDs. GPU MiB means **baseline-subtracted device-used peak**, not the shim cap\nand not the absolute device total. Each coordinate has three independent rounds;\nmedians are fitted with equal weight. Source data are immutable inputs. Supporting\nrecords include D023's probe paths, D014's static calibration job, D019's operation\nIDs (not invented lab job IDs), D011/D009 clean screen jobs, D015's job and its\nconflicting repetition caveat, exact header-derived weight bytes for all six\nrepresentations, and the three existing hybrid transform manifests. A null job ID\nis explicitly explained where the source uses an operation or probe instead.\n\nThe static model is ordinary least squares:\n\n```text\nGPU MiB(K)       = 1387.600000 - 26.933333 K\nDecode us(K)     = 1575.237082 + 268.831188 K\nPrompt ms(K)     = 174.238070 + 2.933955 K\n```\n\nThe LRU model is ordinary least squares of log time:\n\n```text\nGPU MiB(C)       = 742 + C\nlog Decode us(C) = 10.290775 - 0.004155091 C\nlog Prompt ms(C) = 5.474944 + 0.000019467 C\n```\n\nThe log model preserves positive time and captures the cache trend with two\nparameters; its error is large. No polynomial or perfectly interpolating fit\nis used to hide that error. Decode t/s = 1e6 / decode us. Prompt ms comes from\nD022's measured 62-token prompt-eval counts/rates, not the much faster bench\npp512 workload. TTFT is **warm prompt ms + one decode step**, excluding load,\nqueueing, tokenization and sampling. Actual first-token arrival was not measured.\nA different prompt length is a linear-scaling hypothesis requiring the flag.\n\n| Family | Max fitted GPU error | Max held-out GPU error | Max fitted decode error | Max held-out decode error |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Static | 0.800 MiB | 2.000 MiB | 7.201% | 16.246% |\n| LRU | <0.001 MiB | <0.001 MiB | 10.327% | 39.263% |\n\nLeave-one-**coordinate**-out removes all three samples of one layer/cache setting\nbefore refitting. It does not leak other repetitions of the held-out setting.\nEndpoint folds test extrapolation for validation only; normal inference refuses\nout-of-domain predictions. The complete per-fold residuals and coefficients are\nreturned in JSON. Prompt-time held-out errors reach ~2.203 ms static and ~5.311 ms\nLRU; these are not TTFT confidence intervals. Budget checks add the maximum\nheld-out GPU residual to predicted device memory. This is an empirical guard,\nnot a statistical guarantee or a bound on other-process interference.\n\nThe calibrated GPU/decode domain is the exact x86-main CPU/GPU/discrete-memory\nprofile, matching Granite mixE4K byte layout, f16 KV, ctx4096, batch2048,\nubatch512, eight threads, the D022 prompt and K in [0,24] or C in [128,640].\nStatic intermediate integer K is interpolation. Every other representation,\nmodel, host, context, KV type, prompt length, batch/ubatch, and all hybrid GPU\npredictions require `--allow-extrapolation` and have `confidence=extrapolated`.\nThe overall `calibrated` label describes the GPU/decode regime only;\n`metric_confidence` separately identifies host as an analytical working-set\nhypothesis and TTFT as a proxy. No flag creates missing measurements.\n\nOutside that domain, byte accounting adjusts the D022 footprint by changed\nresident expert/nonexpert bytes, KV bytes relative to 192 MiB, and a hypothetical\ncompute allowance `108 * ubatch/512`. This assumes linear buffer scaling and\nignores alignment/backend algorithm changes. Static decode transfers use clean\nD009 GPU endpoints where available and scale CPU penalty with expert bytes;\nmissing endpoints fall back to D022's ~684 t/s, explicitly unvalidated. The\nother-representation LRU transfer scales fitted time with expert bytes. These\ncoarse proposals exist to select tests; they are not calibrated speed results.\n\nHybrid candidates use the **existing** n18/n12/n6 split files, exact per-layer\nhot counts/IDs from the transform manifests, and one null expert per layer.\nTheir real-hot totals 168/360/552 plus 24 nulls require 162/324/486 MiB of expert\nGPU payload. If the input includes exactly one complete decode trace group,\nfixed TRAIN-selected IDs are evaluated against its frequencies; they are never\nreranked on evaluation. Otherwise uniform routing is an explicitly stated\nfallback. The serial hypothesis adds `30.6 us * expected misses + 15.2 us * 24`\nto the fitted GPU baseline. This comes only from D019's exploratory harness,\nwith D014's independent 33.3 us calibration as a cross-check; it omits the real\nsplit engine's extra launches and synchronization. The prediction is likely\noptimistic. D027 records a focused CUDA correctness failure: candidates remain\nexploratory until repaired and requalified. CPU equality is insufficient.\n\n## Ranking, quality and the measurement handoff\n\nPlans must fit `predicted GPU + guard <= GPU budget` and conservative host\nworking set `<= host budget`, each capped by physical capacity and optionally\nresource availability. Ranking first prefers the calibrated regime. Within each\nconfidence tier, decode maximizes decode t/s, ttft minimizes the warm TTFT proxy,\nand balanced maximizes decode t/s divided by TTFT. Ties prefer smaller GPU\nfootprint and stable candidate ID. No cross-tier speed claim is implied.\n\nWithout `--max-kld`, measured representation KLD is a risk prior; changed\nCPU/GPU placement, hybrid arithmetic and quantized KV have **unknown combined\nKLD**. They get `quality_class=combined-quality-unmeasured` and `kld=null`.\nA finite quality constraint fails closed on unknown combined KLD even with the\nextrapolation flag. Only a matched original all-GPU representation with f16 KV\ncan use its measured representation drift as the gate. That measurement remains\na wikitext screening prior, not a guarantee for other tasks or context lengths.\nThe 900 MiB example consequently has no admissible plan under a strict KLD\nceiling until placement quality is measured. Unknown quality is never zero.\n\nThe emitted JSON is exactly a mapping of test names to `bin/lab` definitions,\nwith `tool`, `parser`, `model_file`, `docker_args`, `args`, and, for quality,\n`needs_data`. Tests exercise the real `load_tests()` in isolation and compare\nconsumer-required fields with the repository's definitions. No new schema\nfields or automatic modifications of `lab/tests` are introduced.\n\nFor each plan, test prefix is `pl_` plus the first 12 hex characters of SHA256\nof the plan ID. Suffixes are `_engagement` (8 generated tokens), `_timing` (160),\nand, for known Granite variants with the existing logits present, `_quality512`.\nCompletion tests mount the exact D022 chat prompt read-only; model flags match\nthe plan, with `-m` provided once by the runner. Quality uses the same\nplacement/KV flags at **ctx512** with 20 chunks and the existing\n`artifacts/kl/bf16.kl`, as required by that reference. It tests placement drift\nat ctx512; it cannot certify ctx4096/long-context KV quality. Provide the exact\nwikitext-2 `wiki.test.raw` from the D009 job as the job's data input. To validate\nquality at ctx4096, first make matching BF16 reference logits and a paired test\nat that context; this task does not run or invent that measurement.\n\nThe owner must import definitions into a permitted lab location, choose an\nimmutable qualified image, and submit with `--model` in the directory containing\nthe test's `model_file`. Standard representations share the original GGUF\ndirectory; hybrid models live in `artifacts/codexR/models`, requiring a separate\njob/model directory and the hybrid image. Do not mix those directory groups in\none job. Before any scored timing: pass engagement with a backend observation,\nresolve the hybrid CUDA failure if applicable, pass paired quality, bind\nimage/model/prompt hashes, and confirm the predicted footprint. Run three\nalternating forward/reverse timing rounds under the established one-GPU-job,\nno-compile policy. Record shim peak and device delta separately; shim limits are\ncooperative intercepted-allocation limits and exclude ~310 MiB driver/context\noverhead (D023). Replan after measured failure or material prediction error.\nNo tests were submitted or executed on the GPU by this worker.\n\n## Example and remaining gaps\n\nThe real mixE4K inspection at 900 MiB, ctx4096 recommends static n19 with f16 KV:\n875.867 MiB predicted GPU plus 2.000 MiB guard, 1178.695 MiB host working-set\nallowance, 149.633 decode t/s, and 236.666 ms warm TTFT proxy. The hand-derived\nfixture differs by 0.0055 MiB from exact nonexpert header bytes. Exact flags:\n\n```text\n-m /home/muk/models/campaign/gguf/granite-3.1-1b-a400m-instruct-mixE4K.gguf\n-ngl 99 --fit off -c 4096 -b 2048 -ub 512 -ctk f16 -ctv f16\n-t 8 -tb 8 --n-cpu-moe 19\n```\n\nThe remaining top five use K20..23 with progressively lower footprint and\nthroughput. They are predictions between the measured K18 and K24 points,\nwith unmeasured placement KLD. Gaps: actual TTFT/load time, long-context and\nquantized-KV quality, uncontented host working-set/SSD behavior, other model and\nhost calibration, batch/prefill buffer measurements, GPU hybrid correctness,\nlaunch/transfer costs and engagement, disjoint task-level quality, and prediction\nuncertainty beyond the observed residuals. Measurements must decide acceptance.\n# Resource observation contract (draft for x86 and Jetson)\n\n`observe.py` is read-only, stdlib-only Python 3.12. It does not launch workloads,\nresolve containers, modify clocks/power, or reset cumulative counters. It is\nindependent of `bin/lab`. The initial explicit backends are `nvidia-smi` (discrete\nNVIDIA) and `linux` (procfs and cgroup v2). Both are enabled by default. Backend\nselection is explicit through the Python API or repeated CLI `--backend` options.\nUnsupported platforms retain the common fields with unavailable/error status.\n\n## API and output\n\n```python\nfrom observe import sample, sample_series\n\nreading = sample(backends=('nvidia-smi', 'linux'), gpu='0',\n                 cgroup_path='/sys/fs/cgroup/my-job', pid=123)\nfor record in sample_series(.25, duration=10, cgroup_path='/sys/fs/cgroup/my-job'):\n    consume(record)\n# Alternative: until=time.monotonic() + 10 (absolute monotonic seconds).\n```\n\n`sample()` returns a JSON-serializable dictionary:\n\n- `schema`: `resource-sample/0`.\n- `ts`: `{utc: <timezone-aware ISO 8601 string>, monotonic: <seconds>}`,\n  recorded at collection start. Monotonic values are local to this host/boot;\n  they must not be compared between hosts or across reboots.\n- `host_id`: OS hostname (operator-assigned; not a globally unique machine ID).\n- `backends`: ordered list of enabled/attempted backend names. This list does\n  not imply that those backends succeeded; inspect field statuses.\n- `gpu_id`: selected GPU UUID, or null if not obtainable. `gpu` selects one\n  zero-based CSV row index or exact UUID; default `0`. It never sums devices.\n- `fields`: mapping from the common names below to field envelopes.\n- `collection_seconds`: monotonic elapsed collection time. The sample is a\n  sequence of reads, not an atomic snapshot. GPU query subprocesses each have\n  their own timeout (default two seconds).\n\nEvery metric has exactly this envelope:\n\n```json\n{\"value\": null, \"unit\": \"B\", \"source\": \"/sys/fs/cgroup/job/memory.peak\", \"scope\": \"cgroup-subtree\", \"status\": \"unavailable\", \"reason\": \"not found: /sys/fs/cgroup/job/memory.peak\"}\n```\n\n`status` is `ok`, `unavailable`, or `error`. Successful fields have a real value\nand null reason. Missing files, tools, columns, disabled backends, unspecified\nPID/cgroup, empty GPU output, `[N/A]`, and `[Not Supported]` are unavailable\nwith null value and an explanatory reason. Nonzero query exit (including an\nunusable driver), timeouts, permission errors, and malformed numeric readings\nare errors with null value and reason. Missing data is never converted to zero.\nActual measured zero is valid. An empty, successfully read cgroup `io.stat`\nmeans no block-device entries, so its sum is a genuine zero. No fallback from\nmissing `memory.peak` to `memory.current` is made.\n\n## Common metrics\n\n| Field | Unit | Source | Scope / meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `gpu.memory.used` | MiB | NVIDIA `memory.used` | `device-total`; all allocations observed by the driver |\n| `gpu.memory.total` | MiB | NVIDIA `memory.total` | `device-total`; device framebuffer capacity |\n| `gpu.util` | % | NVIDIA `utilization.gpu` | `device-total`; NVIDIA's utilization sampling window |\n| `gpu.temp` | degC | NVIDIA `temperature.gpu` | `device-total` |\n| `gpu.power` | W | NVIDIA `power.draw` | `device-total`; reported draw, not integrated energy |\n| `gpu.clocks.sm` | MHz | NVIDIA `clocks.sm` / CSV `clocks.current.sm` | `device-total` |\n| `gpu.clocks.memory` | MHz | NVIDIA `clocks.mem` / CSV `clocks.current.memory` | `device-total` |\n| `gpu.compute_apps` | records | NVIDIA compute-apps CSV, filtered by UUID | `per-process-on-device`; see below |\n| `host.mem.total` | B | `/proc/meminfo:MemTotal` | `host`; kernel-visible capacity |\n| `host.mem.available` | B | `/proc/meminfo:MemAvailable` | `host`; kernel estimate, not simply free memory |\n| `host.mem.swap.total` | B | `/proc/meminfo:SwapTotal` | `host`; configured swap capacity |\n| `host.mem.swap.free` | B | `/proc/meminfo:SwapFree` | `host`; free swap capacity |\n| `cgroup.memory.current` | B | given cgroup's `memory.current` | `cgroup-subtree`; current charged memory |\n| `cgroup.memory.peak` | B | `memory.peak` | `cgroup-subtree`; kernel peak since creation/reset, not series peak |\n| `cgroup.memory.swap.current` | B | `memory.swap.current` | `cgroup-subtree`; current swap charge |\n| `cgroup.memory.swap.peak` | B | `memory.swap.peak` | `cgroup-subtree`; kernel swap peak where supported |\n| `cgroup.io.rbytes` | B | `io.stat` sum of device `rbytes` | `cgroup-subtree`; cumulative block read bytes, not a rate |\n| `process.rss` | B | `/proc/<pid>/status:VmRSS` | `process`; kernel RSS estimate, including shared pages |\n\nThe `gpu.clocks` family uses separate `.sm` and `.memory` envelopes so one clock\ncan be unsupported without hiding the other. Source strings identify the exact\nquery field or file; procfs kB and NVIDIA MiB use binary multiples (1024 and\n1048576 bytes respectively). GPUs retain native MiB; host/cgroup/process values\nare converted to bytes. Counts and byte totals retain integer precision.\n\n`gpu.compute_apps.value` is a list of `{pid: <positive integer>, used_memory:\n<field envelope in MiB>}`. Per-process used memory can be unavailable while the\nPID remains known. A successful header-only apps CSV yields an empty list;\nempty output without a header is unavailable. The global apps query is filtered\nby the selected GPU UUID, preventing attribution to a different device. No GPU\nUUID means unavailable attribution. These are compute processes only, not an\nexhaustive list of graphics/driver allocations. PIDs are as reported by the\nNVIDIA driver and can differ from PIDs visible in a container namespace.\n\n## Attribution and interpretation\n\nDevice totals include other processes, contexts, and driver overhead. A difference\nfrom a baseline is not proof of per-job GPU allocation; jobs and background\nactivity may overlap. Do not label device-total peaks as per-process peaks.\nCompute-app memory does not necessarily sum to device-used memory, and it must\nnot be added to that total.\n\nCgroup observations require the caller to provide the actual cgroup v2 path.\nThey describe the subtree, not exclusively a single PID. They do **not** account\nfor driver/GPU allocations as a complete GPU accounting mechanism. Do not add\nprocess RSS to cgroup current, or child cgroup counters to an ancestor already\nincluding the child. RSS includes shared pages and cannot safely be summed as\nunique physical memory. PID reuse, process exit, and cgroup deletion can race\nsampling; no stable process identity or container resolver is provided.\n\nOn Jetson, CPU and GPU share DRAM. Host RAM use and GPU-associated shared DRAM\nmust **not** be summed as independent physical consumption. NVIDIA discrete\nframebuffer fields must not be filled with Jetson RAM values. Keep those common\nGPU memory fields unavailable unless a backend can provide their stated\nsemantics. Add shared-memory observations under platform fields with\n`scope=shared-dram` and explicit overlap metadata/documentation.\n\n## Series and loss accounting\n\n`sample_series(interval, duration=None, *, until=None, sampler=sample, ...)`\nis a lazy iterator. Exactly one of `duration` (nonnegative seconds) and `until`\n(absolute monotonic deadline) is required; interval must be finite and positive.\nIt yields each sample as soon as collected, followed by one\n`resource-series/0` summary. Fully consume the iterator to obtain the summary.\nA zero duration/past deadline produces only a zero-count summary.\n\nTicks are scheduled from the start at fixed intervals in `[start, end)`; the\nfirst tick is immediate. Delays skip elapsed ticks rather than replaying a burst\nof stale measurements. The latest due tick receives the next collection. Sample\nstarts must precede the deadline; a collection started before it can finish\nafterward. A short window can end collection after its last scheduled tick\nwithout sleeping to the end. Slow JSONL consumers contribute to delay and loss.\n\nThe summary contains:\n\n- `start_monotonic`, `end_monotonic` (requested deadline), `finished_monotonic`,\n  and `interval_seconds`.\n- `expected_samples`, `obtained_samples`, and `lost_samples` where expected =\n  obtained + lost. Expected counts all scheduled ticks, including those missed\n  during overruns and failed sampler calls.\n- `max_gap_seconds`: maximum separation between obtained collection starts;\n  null with fewer than two samples. `leading_gap_seconds` and\n  `trailing_gap_seconds` expose boundary coverage, including a full window\n  without samples. The trailing gap is relative to the requested deadline.\n- `collection_errors`: slot and reason for sampler exceptions. A returned\n  sample with unavailable/error fields still counts as an obtained sample;\n  this counter measures delivery loss, not metric availability.\n\nNo interpolation, invented measurements, or burst catch-up is performed.\nInterrupted/abandoned iterators and CLI Ctrl-C do not produce a final summary;\nconsumers must treat a missing summary as incomplete. SIGINT exits 130.\n\n## CLI\n\n```sh\npython3 -B cc/resource_observe/observe.py once --out jsonl\npython3 -B cc/resource_observe/observe.py watch --interval .25 --duration 10 --out jsonl\npython3 -B cc/resource_observe/observe.py once --backend linux --pid 123 --cgroup-path /sys/fs/cgroup/job\n```\n\n`--out jsonl` chooses the format; records go to stdout. The observer does not\nopen an output path. Redirect stdout only to an authorized location. `watch`\ndefaults to ten seconds and supports `--until` instead of `--duration`.\nField errors remain data and do not fail the CLI. Invalid arguments exit 2.\n\n## Platform extension and Jetson handoff\n\nThe Python API accepts `extra_backends={name: zero_arg_callable}` with the name\nincluded explicitly in `backends`. Each callable returns a field mapping under\n`platform.<name>.*`; every field must use the six-key envelope. Extensions cannot\noverwrite common fields. Example:\n\n```python\nsample(backends=('linux', 'jetson'), extra_backends={\n    'jetson': lambda: {\n        'platform.jetson.ram.used': field(123, 'B', 'tegrastats:RAM', 'shared-dram')\n    }\n})\n```\n\nA backend must handle its own tool failures and return status envelopes. An\nuncaught extension exception fails `sample()` and becomes a collection error\nwhen called through `sample_series()`. The current CLI exposes only the two\nbuilt-in backends; the Jetson lead should register its backend and CLI choice\nwhen it lands. Common field semantics require a contract/schema revision if\nchanged; new namespaced fields are additive under `resource-sample/0`.\n\nJetson should implement a read-only `tegrastats` backend with bounded process\nlifetime and timeout, real host captures, malformed/missing version variants,\nand tests. Suggested platform fields (preserve raw label/source and unsupported\nstatus by Jetson model/version):\n\n| Family | Suggested observations | Unit / scope |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `platform.jetson.ram.*` | RAM used/total, largest free block count/size | B (count for block count), `shared-dram` |\n| `platform.jetson.swap.*` | used/total/cached | B, `host` |\n| `platform.jetson.gr3d.*` | GPU activity and each reported frequency | %, MHz, `device-total` |\n| `platform.jetson.emc.*` | memory-controller activity/frequency | %, MHz, `shared-dram` |\n| `platform.jetson.cpu.*` | per-core activity, frequency, offline state | %, MHz, boolean; `host` |\n| `platform.jetson.temperature.*` | named thermal sensors | degC, named sensor scope |\n| `platform.jetson.power.*` | named rails, instantaneous and reported average | W after explicit mW conversion, named rail scope |\n| `platform.jetson.accelerator.*` | optional VIC/NVENC/NVDEC/NVDLA/PVA activity/clocks | %, MHz, named engine scope |\n\nDo not infer GPU allocated bytes from GR3D utilization or shared RAM use. Keep\nrail averages distinct from instantaneous power; do not sum overlapping rails.\nThe Jetson lead must define exact available labels, overlap relationships, unit\nconversions, and any common-field mapping using its target's tool output. This\nhandoff lists implementation targets, not verified Jetson capabilities.\n\n## Fixtures and verification\n\nRun locally, without network or GPU workloads:\n\n```sh\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider cc/resource_observe/tests\n```\n\n`tests/fixtures/capture.json` records the UTC capture time, this host's hostname,\nexact NVIDIA argv, and exit codes. `meminfo-real.txt` is this host's unmodified\n`/proc/meminfo`. `nvidia-*-real.csv` and their `.stderr` companions are the exact\nquery outputs from this host, including failures; a `.csv` suffix does not imply\na successful CSV response. On this capture both NVIDIA queries exited 9 because\nthe driver could not be contacted. Successful real GPU telemetry was therefore\nunavailable. No real success capture was fabricated or substituted from another\nhost. `*-synthetic.csv`, `*-unsupported.csv`, `*-malformed.*`, `empty.txt`, and\n`nvidia-absent.json` are explicitly synthetic parser/failure cases. Re-capture\nsuccessful real GPU output on a host with a usable driver before treating the\nNVIDIA success path as hardware-validated.\n# Q8 activation reuse campaign patch\n\nApply only to llama.cpp `31288c9c66e59377f7788d920678a565bb89dd37`. See [DESIGN.md](DESIGN.md) for the safety rule and exclusions. This directory contains the patch and its handoff documentation; no reference-source files were edited.\n\n## Build CC\n\nFrom the campaign root, the orchestrator can build with the existing Build CC:\n\n```bash\npython3 cc/build_engine/build_engine.py \\\n  --source ref/llama.cpp --commit 31288c9c6 \\\n  --patch cc/q8_reuse/q8-activation-reuse.patch \\\n  --tag campaign/llamacpp:c1-q8-reuse \\\n  --out artifacts/codexH/build-q8-reuse\n```\n\nUse a fresh/empty output directory and the Build CC's required locally available, digest-pinned CUDA bases. Build a separate unpatched image from the same commit/toolchain for baseline comparison. The Build CC archives the source and applies the patch to its staging copy. Docker and CUDA were not run for this assignment.\n\n## A/B switch\n\nReuse defaults ON. The environment setting is read once per process. Start separate processes for each run:\n\n```bash\nGGML_CUDA_Q8_REUSE=0 /app/llama-bench -m \"$MODEL_Q8\" -ngl 99 -p 0 -n 128 -b 1 -ub 1\nGGML_CUDA_Q8_REUSE=1 /app/llama-bench -m \"$MODEL_Q8\" -ngl 99 -p 0 -n 128 -b 1 -ub 1\n```\n\nThese commands assume the patched tools and model are available at the indicated runtime paths. When launching through Docker, forward the switch with `-e GGML_CUDA_Q8_REUSE=0` or `=1`. An unset switch enables reuse; other values, including `false`, also enable it. Keep model, workload, clocks, threads and all flags identical across runs. Record warmup and repeated decode measurements; do not infer a gain from the patch alone.\n\n## Required validation before acceptance\n\n1. Compile the patch with nvcc in the target Build CC. Run `test-backend-ops` for CUDA/CPU matmul correctness, including one-token Q8_0 and control types/batches. No CUDA compile result is available yet.\n2. Compare the unpatched Q8_0 baseline, patched OFF and patched ON with identical model/corpus/options. The assignment requires mean KLD **0** and **100% same top-1** using `llama-perplexity --kl-divergence-base`. Force `-b 1 -ub 1` so the check exercises the changed decode path, rather than only prompt MMQ. For example, with host paths to the built binaries:\n\n   ```bash\n   \"$BASELINE_BIN/llama-perplexity\" -m \"$MODEL_Q8\" -f \"$CORPUS\" \\\n     -ngl 99 -c 128 -b 1 -ub 1 --chunks 4 \\\n     --kl-divergence-base artifacts/codexH/baseline.kld\n\n   GGML_CUDA_Q8_REUSE=0 \"$PATCHED_BIN/llama-perplexity\" \\\n     -m \"$MODEL_Q8\" -f \"$CORPUS\" -ngl 99 -c 128 -b 1 -ub 1 --chunks 4 \\\n     --kl-divergence-base artifacts/codexH/baseline.kld --kl-divergence\n\n   GGML_CUDA_Q8_REUSE=1 \"$PATCHED_BIN/llama-perplexity\" \\\n     -m \"$MODEL_Q8\" -f \"$CORPUS\" -ngl 99 -c 128 -b 1 -ub 1 --chunks 4 \\\n     --kl-divergence-base artifacts/codexH/baseline.kld --kl-divergence\n   ```\n\n   **Tool limitation:** this revision saves clipped, uint16-quantized log probabilities, not lossless raw logits (`tools/perplexity/perplexity.cpp`, the two `log_softmax` overloads). A baseline-versus-itself KLD run may therefore report a small nonzero mean and quantization can obscure top-1 ties. Run that self-comparison too. Do not declare bit identity from a rounded KLD number or silently waive the requested zero gate. Report discrepancies and the self-comparison floor to the orchestrator.\n\n3. Independently compare every raw F32 logit byte against baseline and patched OFF using a lossless logits-dumping harness (`llama_get_logits_ith` for each evaluated token). Require byte-for-byte equality, not a tolerance. Include repeated one-token evaluations with changing inputs/positions, enough calls for graph warmup/capture/replay, and graph/shape switches that grow scratch sizes then return to an older captured graph. Repeat with `GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_GRAPHS=1` and with its variable **unset** to enable graphs; this backend treats any set value, including `0`, as disabling graphs.\n4. Exercise allocator alias hazards in backend graphs: repeated use of one input, distinct tensor identities sharing an address, partial-overlap output views, an intervening writer reusing input storage, and successive evaluations that reuse the same tensor object/address with new values. Verify that overlapping RoPE, successful fusion, other operations, and concurrent-stream graphs cannot reuse a stale entry. Check MoE and fused paths as unchanged controls.\n5. Profile kernel launches and decode throughput for ON versus OFF. In an unfused Q/RoPE/K/V safe window with the same input, expect one quantization instead of three. Fused operations and other writers end the window, so model-level savings must be measured. Check no regression in disabled, non-Q8_0 and multi-token paths. Capture/replay and pointer-lifetime checks are mandatory even if direct execution passes.\n\n## Checks performed here\n\n- Confirmed reference HEAD is the target commit and its tracked working tree is clean.\n- `git apply --check --verbose` passed on a `git archive` copy under `artifacts/codexH/verification-tree`, with Git discovery stopped before the enclosing campaign repository. All three intended files were checked.\n- Applied the patch to that copy and compared all three resulting files against the edited work copies; reverse application check passed.\n- Host smoke checks passed with C++17, `-Wall -Wextra -Werror`, AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, for unset, `0`, `1` and `false` switch settings. LeakSanitizer was disabled because it cannot run under the sandbox's ptrace. These mocks do not validate CUDA compilation or GPU arithmetic.\n\nEvidence and repeatable host checks: `artifacts/codexH/validation.txt`, `host_check.py`, `host_check.cpp`, and `make_patch.py`. Runtime numerical/performance gates remain pending with the orchestrator.\n# Quantized KV scheduler capacity fix\n\nBase: llama.cpp `31288c9c66e59377f7788d920678a565bb89dd37`.\nPatch: `cc/kv_quant_fix/kv-quant-fix.patch`.\nMechanism and CPU results: `artifacts/codexKVB/ROOTCAUSE.md`.\n\nThe patch sizes the scheduler's tensor hash table for both nodes and leaves, matching `ggml_new_graph_custom`. It changes allocation capacity only; the compute graph and KV quantization are unchanged.\n\n## GPU handoff (orchestrator only)\n\nRun from the campaign root with the campaign's locally available CUDA base images. Build CC resolves and records their digests; `BASE_DEVEL` and `BASE_RUNTIME` can select existing digest-pinned images. No GPU build or Docker command was executed during this assignment.\n\n```bash\nnice -n 10 python3 cc/kv_quant_fix/build-cc-j4.py \\\n  --source ref/llama.cpp \\\n  --commit 31288c9c66e59377f7788d920678a565bb89dd37 \\\n  --patch cc/kv_quant_fix/kv-quant-fix.patch \\\n  --tag campaign/llamacpp:c2-kv-quant-fix \\\n  --out artifacts/codexKVB/build-gpu-fixed\n```\n\nThe wrapper invokes the existing `cc/build_engine/build_engine.py` with a private recipe copy in `artifacts/codexKVB/gpu-recipes-j4`. Its only recipe change is `--parallel 10` to `--parallel 4`. Build CC records the copied recipe's actual hash. It does not alter the shared Build CC files. Wrapper argument parsing was checked with `--help`.\n\nVerify all six cache-type/context combinations with the resulting CUDA image:\n\n```bash\nfor kv_type in f16 q8_0 q4_0; do\n  for context in 4096 32768; do\n    nice -n 10 docker run --rm --network none --gpus all \\\n      -v \"$HOME/models/campaign/gguf:/models:ro\" \\\n      --entrypoint /app/llama-completion campaign/llamacpp:c2-kv-quant-fix \\\n      -m /models/granite-3.1-1b-a400m-instruct-mixE4K.gguf \\\n      -p hi -n 4 -c \"$context\" -fa on -ctk \"$kv_type\" -ctv \"$kv_type\" \\\n      -ngl 99 -t 4 --seed 42 \\\n      > \"artifacts/codexKVB/gpu-${kv_type}-${context}.stdout\" \\\n      2> \"artifacts/codexKVB/gpu-${kv_type}-${context}.stderr\" || exit 1\n  done\ndone\n```\n\nRequire exit status zero, generated tokens, actual CUDA layer offload, and flash-attention enabled in logs. Compare f16 output against an unpatched build using the same seed and toolchain. CUDA kernels, output quality, and multi-GPU pipeline behavior remain GPU verification work.\n\n## CPU replay\n\n```bash\nnice -n 10 cmake --build artifacts/codexKVB/build --target llama-completion -j4\nnice -n 10 python3 artifacts/codexKVB/run_cpu.py after\nnice -n 10 artifacts/codexKVB/test-scheduler-capacity\n```\n\n`run_cpu.py` records exact arguments, environment overrides, return codes, stdout, and stderr per case. Baseline logs were captured before applying the patch; the current scratch source and binary contain the clean patch with diagnostics removed.\n# Placement-aware expert precision (T-PAP)\n\nThis recipe quantizes only the experts assigned to CPU at lower precision, while keeping the other experts at Q4_K and ordinary quantizable weights at Q8_0. It is a model-production technique; it needs no inference-engine changes. Norm vectors and expert router matrices remain F32 under upstream quantization policy (`src/llama-quant.cpp:295-314`).\n\nThe scratch source is a `git archive` of `ref/llama.cpp` commit `31288c9c66e59377f7788d920678a565bb89dd37`, under `artifacts/codexPAP/tree`. The build is CPU-only, uses `nice -n 10`, and limits compilation and quantization to four threads. No Docker or network is used by the recipe. The evaluation JSON is an orchestrator handoff and does not execute Docker here.\n\nThe initial calibration build's automatically generated banner used the parent campaign Git hash `bc22a4f`; its source was still the pinned archive. The recipe explicitly sets `LLAMA_BUILD_COMMIT` and archive build number 0 to prevent this cosmetic provenance error. Initial configure/build logs and the calibration executable hash are retained; the tools are rebuilt with the explicit stamp before model production.\n\n## Integration conditions\n\nUse each `papK_*` with `-ngl 99 --n-cpu-moe K`. In the archived source, `common/arg.cpp:2771-2777` documents the first N layers and calls `llm_add_n_cpu_ffn_overrides`; `common/common.h:1255-1261` actually loops over indices `0 <= i < N`, creating CPU buffer overrides. These citations refer to the pinned scratch source, not an arbitrary later upstream version. GPU-capable evaluation builds must preserve those semantics. Changing K at inference changes which device gets the low-bit weights and invalidates a placement-matched comparison. A CPU-only run cannot measure the intended CPU/GPU division.\n\nThe recipe places the specific first-K-layer regex before `ffn_.*_exps=q4_K`. Repeated `--tensor-type REGEX=TYPE` arguments are parsed at `tools/quantize/quantize.cpp:317-347`; `src/llama-quant.cpp:774-785` uses `std::regex_search` and breaks on the first match. Types are case-insensitive. Every output is quantized directly from the read-only BF16 source, with Q8_0 as the base type. No low-bit output is requantized from another low-bit model.\n\n| Output basename | Layers 0 through K-1 | Layers K through 23 | Imatrix |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| pap12_q3.gguf | 0-11 Q3_K | 12-23 Q4_K | TRAIN |\n| pap12_q2.gguf | 0-11 Q2_K | 12-23 Q4_K | TRAIN |\n| pap18_q3.gguf | 0-17 Q3_K | 18-23 Q4_K | TRAIN |\n| pap18_q2.gguf | 0-17 Q2_K | 18-23 Q4_K | TRAIN |\n| pap24_q3.gguf | 0-23 Q3_K | none | TRAIN |\n| pap24_q2.gguf | 0-23 Q2_K | none | TRAIN |\n| pap24_q3_noimat.gguf | 0-23 Q3_K | none | none |\n| mix4_imat.gguf | all Q4_K | all Q4_K | TRAIN |\n\n`pap24_q3_noimat` is the mixE3K-equivalent recipe control. `mix4_imat` isolates the effect of calibration at four bits and is evaluated at all three K values. The existing `granite-3.1-1b-a400m-instruct-mixE4K.gguf` is an external prerequisite, evaluated at K=12,18,24. It is not copied into the output directory; the orchestrator must include that basename in the common model directory.\n\n## Reproduction and provenance\n\nRun `cc/pap/make_pap.sh` from any directory. It builds, calibrates, quantizes eight models, verifies actual GGUF tensors using the pinned gguf-py reader, hashes the files, and emits the manifest and lab tests. Individual stages are `build`, `calibrate`, `quantize`, and `verify`. The optional `sanity` stage runs only the CPU pap12_q3 KLD check; run `verify` afterward to include its result in the manifest. Re-running a stage overwrites that stage's outputs. Python requires the already installed NumPy; CMake and the local C/C++ toolchain are used without dependency downloads. Python bytecode writes outside the assignment roots are disabled.\n\nCalibration command (paths expanded in the script):\n\n```sh\nnice -n 10 artifacts/codexPAP/build/bin/llama-imatrix \\\n  -m ~/models/campaign/gguf/granite-3.1-1b-a400m-instruct-bf16.gguf \\\n  -f ~/models/campaign/data/wikitext/wikitext-2-raw/wiki.train.raw \\\n  -o artifacts/codexPAP/models/granite-wtrain.imatrix \\\n  --output-format gguf -t 6 -tb 6 -ngl 0 -c 512 --chunks 100 --no-ppl \\\n  --output-frequency 10\n```\n\nThe imatrix uses only the first 100 processed TRAIN chunks; TEST is not used for calibration. It is GGUF format despite the requested `.imatrix` suffix. `MANIFEST.json` records the imatrix metadata, completed chunks, TRAIN and TEST SHA256 hashes, source and output hashes, sizes, and each tensor's actual type and dimensions. `logs/imatrix.log` records the run; calibration start/end files record UTC timestamps. The train/test distinction is dataset provenance, not a claim of zero overlapping natural-language strings.\n\n## Evaluation handoff\n\n`artifacts/codexPAP/lab-tests-pap.json` contains 22 tests using exactly the existing lab entry shapes. All `model_file` references are basenames.\n\nThe orchestrator must register this JSON in the lab's test-definition directory, place the eight outputs and existing mixE4K baseline in one model directory, and supply `--data ~/models/campaign/data/wikitext/wikitext-2-raw/wiki.test.raw` when submitting KLD tests. Registration and lab execution are outside this assignment's write locations. Existing absolute read-only prompt/KL mounts match the supplied lab templates.\n\n- Six placement-matched decode runs for calibrated PAP outputs; one for `pap24_q3_noimat` at K=24; three for `mix4_imat`; three for mixE4K. Decode copies `lab/tests/match1.json`: `llama-completion -f /tin/chat.txt -n 160 -c 4096 --temp 0 -s 1 -ngl 99 -no-cnv --no-warmup --n-cpu-moe K`, with the read-only `artifacts/traces/in:/tin:ro` mount.\n- Nine KLD runs, covering every produced model and mixE4K. Each copies `lab/tests/mem2.json`'s `kl_q8`: `llama-perplexity -c 512 --chunks 20 -ngl 99 --kl-divergence-base /out/bf16.kl --kl-divergence`, with `needs_data: true`, parser `kl`, and read-only `artifacts/kl:/out:ro` mount. The lab supplies WikiText-2 TEST through its standard data handling. Saved KL data also carries its evaluation tokens.\n\nThe full GPU lab suite is defined here for orchestrator execution. Any optional CPU sanity outcome is recorded separately in `artifacts/codexPAP/cpu-sanity.json` and in the manifest; it is not a substitute for the suite. The BF16 KL header is checked against the pinned reader's format (`tools/perplexity/perplexity.cpp:1688-1714`).\n\n## Claims allowed\n\nVerified type placement, byte reductions, calibration provenance, and content hashes are factory properties. The hypothesis is that smaller CPU expert weights reduce DRAM traffic during decode; speedup need not scale with file size because kernels, routing, caching, and GPU work also contribute. File size does not establish resident host RAM or VRAM. Calibration is intended to reduce low-bit quality loss; that benefit must be measured against the controls. Do not claim throughput improvement, memory residency savings, acceptable quality for all variants, or an imatrix quality improvement without completed corresponding lab results. A WikiText CPU sanity KLD, if present, supports only that model and dataset, not task-level quality.\n\n## Delivered measurements\n\nAll eight GGUFs passed the gguf-py tensor verification. Sizes below include GGUF metadata; complete SHA256 hashes and all 242 tensor records per model are in `artifacts/codexPAP/MANIFEST.json`.\n\n| File | Bytes | MiB |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| pap12_q3.gguf | 738,044,192 | 703.854 |\n| pap12_q2.gguf | 676,702,496 | 645.354 |\n| pap18_q3.gguf | 697,936,160 | 665.604 |\n| pap18_q2.gguf | 605,923,616 | 577.854 |\n| pap24_q3.gguf | 657,828,128 | 627.354 |\n| pap24_q2.gguf | 535,144,736 | 510.354 |\n| pap24_q3_noimat.gguf | 657,827,808 | 627.353 |\n| mix4_imat.gguf | 818,260,256 | 780.354 |\n| granite-wtrain.imatrix | 16,787,104 | 16.009 |\n\nImatrix SHA256: `106f834b2452b33a0a4eed7d69270a60c5b2330879f2c292a5331bc8450f77a9`. Calibration ran from 2026-11-09T23:08:34Z to 2026-11-09T23:14:28Z, with 100 chunks of 512 tokens and all 32 experts observed for every expert tensor. TRAIN SHA256: `6707892fa3788b5ab9ed78ab5ff37d9fe825f6011a2ad4fcd6a6d467f0e7da57`. TEST SHA256: `173c87a53759e0201f33e0ccf978e510c2042d7f2cb78229d9a50d79b9e7dd08`.\n\nThe optional CPU pap12_q3 check completed successfully against the existing BF16 KL reference (20 chunks, context 512, six inference threads). Mean KLD was **0.072459 +/- 0.002828**, PPL(Q) **10.747627**, PPL(base) **10.006522**, and PPL ratio **1.074062**. Same-top-token agreement in the raw log was **86.961 +/- 0.472%**. This is a finite-statistics sanity result, with no quality acceptance threshold. It does not establish a calibration improvement because the corresponding controls have not been evaluated here. The exact command and timestamps are in `cpu-sanity.json`; the raw output is in `logs/cpu-sanity.log`.\n\nRemaining work for the orchestrator: register and execute the 22 GPU lab tests, provide the existing mixE4K baseline beside the outputs, and compare runtime throughput, memory, and quality before making performance or quality claims. No GPU decode or other model KLD result is claimed. Production and verification finished before the 50-minute checkpoint.\n# nsys_decode: per-token decode breakdown from nsys SQLite\n\nRead-only Python standard-library profiler analysis; no CUDA, Docker, nsys\ninstallation, external packages, database mutation, or compilation required.\nThe tests require pytest. Runtime is under two seconds on the supplied traces.\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/nsys_decode/analyze_nsys.py \\\n  artifacts/x4-hs/prof/static-n12.sqlite artifacts/x4-hs/prof/split-n12.sqlite \\\n  --warmup 5 --marker-bytes 196620 --output artifacts/codexPROF/breakdown.json\n\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -m pytest -q cc/nsys_decode/test_analyze_nsys.py \\\n  --basetemp=artifacts/codexPROF/pytest-tmp \\\n  -o cache_dir=artifacts/codexPROF/pytest-cache\n```\n\nEach input must contain one CUDA process, one logits D2H copy per token on the\nselected `--device` (default 0), and the standard nsys StringIds, memcpy-enum,\nruntime and memcpy tables. Kernel/graph/memset tables may be absent. Set\n`--marker-bytes` to this model's logits size; **check the repeating pattern**\nbefore interpreting results. Multiple requests, chunked logits, concurrent\ninference, and unrelated same-sized D2H copies require a different marker.\n\nThe first marker ends prefill; the following intervals are decode steps. Remove\nthe first `--warmup` intervals (default 5); the final marker closes the final\ncomplete token. Wall time includes sampling and scheduling between logits copies.\nIntervals are clipped to boundaries, counts belong to the interval of their\nstart, and memcpy sizes/API times are joined using CUDA correlation IDs.\nAPI timing therefore includes a boundary-crossing call even if its start count\nbelongs to the preceding token. The last synchronization tail after the final\nDMA completion is outside the window.\n\nJSON includes every retained token, numeric mean/median/min/max, API counts and\ndurations, copy kind/size/device/API durations, graph-exec frequencies, and\nkernel rankings. Times are microseconds, bytes are bytes, raw timestamps ns.\n\nGPU busy means the **union of observed graph envelopes, explicit kernels,\nmemcpy and memset intervals**, including all streams on the selected device.\nGraph envelopes include internal device gaps; this is not hardware utilization.\nIdle gaps are the complement within each token (boundary gaps included);\n`--gap-us` additionally reports gaps at least that long (default 10us).\nThe four exclusive wall-time buckets are compute envelope, transfers outside\ncompute, GPU idle inside sync/copy API calls, and remaining GPU idle. Their sum\nis wall time. API duration totals overlap GPU work and must not be added to it.\n\nGraph-level traces hide constituent kernel counts and times. Steady-state\nexplicit kernel rankings may correctly be empty. The separate **first decode**\nranking exposes initialization/uncaptured kernels when present; it is diagnostic,\nnot a steady-state kernel measurement. Host idle outside observed APIs includes\nCPU compute, scheduling, launch submission, sampling and untraced work, and is\nnot a CPU-compute measurement. Compare profiled results with unprofiled timing.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
