{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat cc/engine_handoff/ENVELOPES.md; cat cc/engine_handoff/README.md; rg -n 'claim|representation|computation-preserving' README* decisions/D0{24,28,29,30,31}*; sed -n '1,160p' bin/lab; rg -n '\"'^def |add_argument|emit_lab|runtime|predicted|selected'\"' cc/moe_planner/plan.py; cat lab/jobs/job-ad883810b3/job.json\"","aggregated_output":"# Shared job envelopes (T-S)\n\n`envelopes.py` is a thin offline projection of Build CC records and `bin/lab`\njob/attempt/export records. It does not create a new executor, provision a\nsandbox, contact a receiver, inspect processes, run Docker, or use the network.\nThe other team's lab agent is not exercised by these checks.\n\n## Python and CLI contract\n\nThe three entry points are `make_proposal(build_record, job_spec, host_profile,\npolicy)`, `check_admission(proposal, host_profile)`, and\n`make_result(job_dir, attempt, claim_category=\"unclassified\", *,\nmanifest_path=None)`. They return JSON-compatible dictionaries. Inputs to the\nfirst two functions are dictionaries; `job_spec` accepts either the immutable\n`job.json` wrapper or its `spec`. No argument dictionaries are mutated.\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/engine_handoff/envelopes.py proposal \\\n  --build-record builds/results/b0-baseline-5/build-record.json \\\n  --job-spec lab/jobs/job-ad883810b3/job.json \\\n  --host-profile cc/engine_handoff/hosts/x86-main.json \\\n  --policy cc/engine_handoff/fixtures/envelopes/policy.example.json \\\n  --out artifacts/codexS/proposal-real.json\n\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/engine_handoff/envelopes.py admit \\\n  --proposal artifacts/codexS/proposal-real.json \\\n  --host-profile cc/engine_handoff/hosts/jetson-arm.TEMPLATE.json\n\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/engine_handoff/envelopes.py result \\\n  --job-dir lab/jobs/job-8df3c7bb27 --attempt att-1 \\\n  --claim-category interrupted-observation \\\n  --out artifacts/codexS/result-interrupted.json\n```\n\nAll commands write JSON to stdout unless `--out` is supplied. `admit` returns\n0 for accepted and 2 for rejected or needs-information. Malformed files or\ninconsistent result ownership return 2 with a JSON error on stderr. Generation\nof an unresolved proposal returns 0: inspect `dispatchable` and `unresolved`.\n\nThe example policy is a **new requester declaration**, not recovered historical\npolicy. It specifies whole-job wall time and capture bytes; the original lab's\nper-test `resources.timeout_s` is deliberately not relabelled as a job budget.\nPolicy supplies `campaign_id`, `experiment_id`, `attempt_id`, `release_role`,\n`build_record_ref`, `wall_time_s`, `capture_bytes`, `memory`, and `evidence`.\n`request_id` defaults to the recorded job ID or spec key. Explicit spec IDs take\nprecedence over policy IDs. Unknown IDs are null, never inferred from filenames.\n\n## Proposal fields\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `format` | `campaign-job-proposal/0` |\n| `campaign_id`, `request_id`, `experiment_id`, `attempt_id` | Requester/controller identities |\n| `target.host_id`, `target.platform`, `target.required_capabilities` | Selected receiver and image's required platform/runtime |\n| `target.platform_compat` | `cuda_arch` from CMake, plus optional declared `jetpack_l4t`, `driver_min` from `spec.platform_compat` |\n| `release.role`, `release.source` | Release role, recorded source commit and tree |\n| `release.patch_sha256`, `release.cmake_defines` | Patch identity (null means unpatched), all parsed CMake flags plus schema 2 defines |\n| `release.transport` | `kind: docker-archive`, recorded SHA-256 and bytes |\n| `release.image_config_id`, `release.index_digest` | Separate config/index identities; unavailable index may be null |\n| `release.executables_sha256`, `release.build_record_ref` | Recorded `/app` file hashes (libraries included), record locator |\n| `inputs` | `spec.inputs` model, prompt/data, and canonical test-definition hashes |\n| `invocation.tests` | Ordered test occurrences with argv, environment, declared cache state |\n| `invocation.mounts` | Role, access, optional container target; no host bind paths |\n| `policy` | Whole-job wall/capture budgets, memory domain/budget/guarantee/accounting, `gpu_jobs_on_host: 1` |\n| `evidence` | Controller outside sandbox, durable destination reference, expected output names |\n| `unresolved`, `dispatchable` | Recomputed identity/policy/receiver gaps; dispatchable only when empty |\n\nNested build-record schemas 1 and 2 are accepted. `image.config_digest` is used\nfor config identity; Docker's reported `Id` is never substituted for it. A null\nindex digest is retained rather than fabricated from an archive catalog or an\nuntyped Docker ID. If the job's image ID is not any recorded config/reported/\nindex identity, the build/job pairing is unresolved. This occurs in the specified\nreal records: they are useful fixtures, but are not a matching dispatchable pair.\n\nThe specified jobs are also legacy submissions without `spec.inputs`. Their\n`model_sha256` is not promoted into a pretend full input manifest. Missing input\nidentities reject admission; missing transport SHA requests more information.\nOther unknown required declarations result in needs-information. A known\nplatform, CUDA architecture, driver floor, capability, or memory guarantee\nmismatch rejects admission even if other fields remain unknown. `dispatchable`\nand any sender-supplied flag are never trusted by admission.\n\nA newly pinned proposal can supply `spec.test_definitions`, using the existing\n`bin/lab` definition mapping. Definition identity is the same canonical SHA-256\nas `bin/lab`: sorted keys, compact separators, ASCII JSON. The adapter constructs\n`/app/<tool> -m /m/<model_file>`, optional `-f /d/<data_file>`, and definition args.\nIt preserves occurrence order, including repeated tests. Read-only `-v` and\n`--volume` forms become input roles. Other Docker flags stay unresolved.\n`spec.invocation` may instead explicitly declare the complete invocation.\n\nHost paths from `inputs.mounts` become `prompt-0`, `prompt-1`, etc. in sorted host\nlocator order; file manifests retain relative names and hashes. Data locators\nbecome basenames. Model/data default container targets are `/m` and `/d`; results\nis `/results`. Prompt roles retain their declared container target, e.g. `/tin`.\nAbsolute argv paths must resolve to a hashed input in these mounts (or a results\noutput). Receiver staging resolves roles to its own paths. Build-record and\ndurable-store references remain locators, not bind instructions.\n\n**Trust boundary:** proposal values are copied declarations. This adapter does\nnot re-observe archive bytes or prove model completeness, provenance, authenticity,\nor invocation safety. The existing `engine_handoff.py` archive observation and\nrequirements receipt must still bind delivered bytes before execution. An\naccepted compatibility receipt is neither execution nor test assessment. The\nrequested GPU concurrency of one is not a new GPU lock or capacity reservation.\nCooperative interception requires the right engine/shim and demonstrated runtime\ninterception (D021/D023); a host capability declaration does not prove these.\nCgroup host RAM accounting is not a discrete VRAM guarantee.\n\n## Result fields and evidence limits\n\n`campaign-job-result/0` keeps `execution_outcome`, `test_assessment`, and\n`claim_category` separate. Claim category is caller-supplied, defaults to\n`unclassified`, and is not inferred from `quality.all_ok`. There is no top-level\nsuccess boolean. Historical `quality` is retained as an assessment declaration,\nnot re-scored or upgraded into a correctness/performance claim.\n\n`evidence_completeness` contains numeric `expected`, `started`, `clean`, and a\n`missing` list of expected test occurrence IDs lacking clean completion. It also\nreports `manifest_present` and `journal_errors`. Clean means a durable per-test\nassessment with exit code zero and explicit `partial: false`; it does not mean\nthe test assessor passed. Interrupted legacy attempts with no per-test assessment\nhave zero established clean tests, even when stdout files exist. Partial evidence\nis never rescored; incomplete assessments are marked `assessed: false` while\nretaining recorded quality. Nine tests started in the interrupted real attempt;\nten completed cleanly in its new attempt. Attempts never share coverage or tokens.\n\n`last_durable_sequence` records physical state-journal line count and the last\nvalid record timestamp. Torn/invalid lines are listed and suppress assessment;\nline count is an observed local-file position, not a remote acknowledgement or\nproof of fsync/host-loss durability. `resource_generation` contains attempt and\nSHA-256 of the owner token, never the raw token; conflicting recorded token hashes\nor attempt/job IDs are refused. `stop_confirmed` requires recorded cleanup\nconfirmation and an explicitly empty owned-container list. A cancellation request\nor terminal journal entry alone cannot confirm stop; no new process probe occurs.\n\n`artifact_refs` lists path + SHA-256 **from export MANIFEST.json**, not recomputed\nhashes of an attempt directory. Default lookup follows `lab/jobs/<job>` to\n`lab/export/<job>/<attempt>/MANIFEST.json`; `--manifest` overrides the locator.\nMissing manifests yield no artifact refs and `manifest_present: false`. These are\nrecorded identities, not a fresh export verification. `bin/lab` exports before\nappending its final terminal event, so an exported state journal may validly have\na different hash/length from the live journal read for `last_durable_sequence`.\nController/evidence on the same host protects against sandbox/process loss only.\n\n## Fixture provenance and verification\n\n`fixtures/envelopes/build-record.json` is an identity-field excerpt of\n`builds/results/b0-baseline-5/build-record.json` (large Buildx provenance omitted).\nThe two jobs' job/owner/result/state files and export manifests are byte-for-byte\ncopies of the requested real records; stdout/stderr payloads are not copied.\n`test-definitions.json` copies relevant current lab definitions as supplemental\ndeclarations, not as retrospective proof of legacy input identity. Tests explicitly\nadd hashes and change the proposed image binding in memory for their accepted\ncase. The synthetic prompt hash in that case tests schema plumbing only.\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -m pytest -q cc/engine_handoff \\\n  --basetemp=cc/engine_handoff/.envelopes-test-tmp \\\n  -o cache_dir=cc/engine_handoff/.pytest_cache\n```\n\n## Questions for the lab-agent team\n\n- What JetPack/L4T, driver/runtime compatibility, and memory accounting/enforcement\n  domains can the Jetson receiver attest to? Fill the template before admission.\n- How will the receiver stage role-bound inputs and independently verify the\n  archive/config/executable identities against delivered bytes?\n- What receiver acknowledgement, owner-generation binding, and stop-confirmation\n  evidence will precede retries and prevent stale result delivery?\n- Which service enforces whole-job wall/capture budgets and GPU concurrency, and\n  what durable destination survives receiver host loss?\n# Engine handoff receipt tool\n\n`engine_handoff.py` inspects delivery bytes and requester requirements offline.\nIt never imports an image or launches a workload. Python 3 and PyYAML are\nrequired; pytest is used for tests. Optional GGUF header inspection uses the\nbundled gguf-py and its existing NumPy dependency, without loading model tensors.\nNo network or Docker is used.\n\n## Archive observations\n\n`observe_archive(path)` returns `image_config_id`, `platform`, `layer_count`, and\n`executables` (absolute `/app/...` names mapped to SHA-256). It supports classic\nDocker `manifest.json` archives and OCI `index.json`/`blobs/sha256` archives,\nincluding nested OCI indexes and Docker attestation descriptors. It admits one\nrunnable image; ambiguous multi-image deliveries are rejected.\n\nThe outer archive is indexed by member headers without extraction. Layers are\nstreamed as plain or gzip tars. Only executable regular files under `app/` are\nhashed into the merged view. Later entries override earlier entries; whiteouts\nremove lower files/subtrees, and opaque whiteouts hide lower directory contents\nwithout hiding files added in the same layer. Non-executable replacements and\nsymlinks remove previous regular-file identities. Symlinks and hardlinks are not\nfollowed or included as regular executables. Executable shared libraries count.\nMemory holds headers, paths, hashes, and bounded stream buffers, never a whole\nlayer or executable. Layer tar headers are discarded as they are consumed.\nMetadata documents have an 8 MiB limit. Archive path traversal and duplicate outer\nmembers are rejected.\n\nThe config's bytes must hash to its filename. This config digest is the image\nconfig ID; an OCI index or manifest digest is a different identity. Content-addressed\nlayer blob names are verified, and config `rootfs.diff_ids` are checked against\nthe uncompressed layer bytes when present. This detects corruption outside\n`app/` too. A classic archive without layer digests or diff_ids can still be bound\nto its recomputed outer archive hash, but lacks those additional integrity checks.\n\n## Facts, declarations, and generation\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/engine_handoff/engine_handoff.py facts \\\n  --build-record builds/results/b0-baseline-3/build-record.json \\\n  --archive builds/results/b0-baseline-3/engine-image.docker.tar \\\n  --model-config /home/muk/models/campaign/granite-3.1-1b-a400m-instruct/config.json \\\n  --gguf /home/muk/models/campaign/gguf/granite-3.1-1b-a400m-instruct-bf16.gguf \\\n  --out-dir cc/engine_handoff/example-real\n\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/engine_handoff/engine_handoff.py generate \\\n  --facts cc/engine_handoff/example-real/facts.json \\\n  --requirements cc/engine_handoff/example/requirements.granite.yaml \\\n  --out cc/engine_handoff/example-real/handoff.yaml \\\n  --invocation-argv /app/llama-completion -m MODEL -n 24\n\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/engine_handoff/engine_handoff.py validate \\\n  cc/engine_handoff/example-real/handoff.yaml\n```\n\n`facts` writes JSON and Markdown. Facts have a value, `observed`/`declared`/`missing`\ntag, and source. Nested Build CC schema-version-1 records are normalized, while\nall original claims remain declared. Image config ID, platform, layer count, and\nexecutable hashes are observed independently from the archive, with its path as\nsource. Archive hashes and sizes are recomputed. Identity mismatches go into\n`unresolved`. Other build metadata stays declared without being represented as\nan independently verified identity or an admission requirement.\n\n`generate` fills release identities only from archive observations. Optional\n`--requirements FILE.yaml` supplies handoff ID, release role, model references\nand manifest, workload artifact, GPU/runtime/resource/network requirements, test\nrequests, test policy, and profiling request/tool. Each copied requester value\nhas a `declared` entry in the handoff's `provenance`; archive identity provenance\nremains `observed`. The workload hash is computed from its bytes and tagged\nobserved, while its requester-provided path stays declared. No requester value\ncan override an observed release identity. Requirements workload paths resolve\nrelative to the requirements file. Other artifact references resolve relative\nto the handoff at validation; absolute manifest references are convenient.\n\n`--workload` remains supported when requirements do not supply the artifact.\nRequirements take precedence for fields they supply. Missing fields remain null\nand listed in `unresolved`. A null profiling tool is allowed when profiling is\nexplicitly false. Put options before `--invocation-argv`; everything after it is\na literal argv element. No shell expansion or execution occurs.\n\nWithout GGUF input the six unavailable header facts are listed in `unresolved`.\nHF config bytes serve as a minimal metadata manifest and do not bind model\nweights/tokenizer. Requirements and facts are provenance records, not signed\nattestations or proof of requester authority.\n\n## Validation and boundaries\n\nValidation prints a JSON receipt verdict and returns 0 for `accepted`, 2 for\n`rejected` or `needs-information`. It rehashes archive, model manifest and workload\nand re-observes every locally readable archive. It compares the handoff's config\nID, platform and full executable map directly with those observations, even if\nthe outer hash/size still match. A blob integrity failure identifies the layer\nor config and failed SHA-256 check. It also rereads the normalized build record\nand compares its identity claims. A record's incomplete executable map remains\na discrepancy, even when its listed tools match a subset of the observed map.\n\nInvalid identities, malformed/ambiguous YAML, forbidden execution keys/strings,\nshell launchers, unsupported platform, network access after staging, nonallowlisted\nmounts and policy violations are rejected. Missing readable artifacts or explicit\nunresolved entries require information unless another check rejects the receipt.\nThe allowed mounts are model:ro, workload:ro and results:rw. The lab retains\nprovisioning/isolation ownership and the external durable-evidence/crash/resume\ncontract. Acceptance is a receipt check, separate from job execution or test\nsuccess; the existing `bin/lab` remains the executor.\n\nArchive checks establish byte identity and consistency, not authenticity,\nprovenance, numerical correctness, model completeness, executable safety, GPU\navailability, runtime compatibility or actual isolation/resource enforcement.\nKeep artifacts immutable between inspection and execution; this tool does not\nhold them for a later executor.\n\n## Tests and retained real run\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -m pytest -q cc/engine_handoff \\\n  --basetemp=cc/engine_handoff/.test-tmp \\\n  -o cache_dir=cc/engine_handoff/.pytest_cache\n```\n\nThe original 48 checks remain, updated to expect archive-derived identities.\nThe inert 20 KiB fixture now uses `/app/run`. Additional small tarfile-built\nfixtures cover both layouts, gzip/plain layers, whiteouts and replacements,\nconfig/layer tampering, edited handoff identities with untouched transport\nhash/size, requester provenance, partial requirements and record discrepancies.\n\nThe integration evidence motivating this change is\n`experiments/handoff-real/{facts.json,handoff.yaml,verdict.json}`: generation had\nleft identities null because it treated all build-record identities as claims.\nThe T-K run is retained in `example-real/`, including facts, requirements,\nworkload, generated handoff, verdict and timings. Reproduction is available in\n`artifacts/codexK/run_real.py`; findings are in `artifacts/codexK/REPORT.md`.\nOlder captures in `example/` predate this follow-up; use `example-real/` for the\nretained real-archive observation result and `example/requirements.granite.yaml`\nfor the sample requester declaration.\nrg: README*: No such file or directory (os error 2)\ndecisions/D029-c1-q8reuse-accepted.md:1:# D029 — C1 q8_reuse ACCEPTED: first full-engine computation-preserving gain (+2.1% Q8_0 decode), x86-main\ndecisions/D029-c1-q8reuse-accepted.md:6:Classification: full-engine code gain, computation-preserving, discrete RTX 5070 Ti, Granite, batch-1 decode. Small: it's a launch/redundancy cleanup (D011 predicted 3-4.5%). Not memory-reducing; stacks with every memory technique. Component CC: cc/q8_reuse (integration: env switch, CUDA mmvq path, applies to 31288c9c6).\ndecisions/D028-kv-precision-protocol.md:4:Accept a KV type as \"same accuracy\" if PPL within +1% of f16 (-fa on) AND greedy text identical or (if not) KLD check pending; report GPU delta MiB (nvidia-smi), prefill & decode t/s medians. Classification: representation (KV) trade-off — not an engine code gain.\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"lab — feasibility core of a \"lab\": image(container) + tests -> job -> attempts -> evidence. No UI. Discovery prototype.\n  lab image add NAME REF            register an image by immutable id/digest\n  lab submit --image N --model P --tests a,b --key K [--res cpus=8,memory=16g,timeout_s=300]   idempotent by key; job.json is immutable\n  lab run JOB                       new attempt (retry = new attempt); no GPU admission control (lock removed)\n  lab cancel JOB ATT                request cancellation (confirmed only when owned containers are gone)\n  lab status [JOB]                  states\n  lab reconcile                     inspect non-terminal attempts against REAL processes/containers; never trusts heartbeats\n  lab register JOB ATT              register result into index (idempotent; stale/conflicting rejected)\nStore: $LAB/{images,jobs,index}. Fault injection for recovery tests: LAB_FAULT=die_after_start|die_in_export\n\"\"\"\nimport json, sys, os, re, time, uuid, hashlib, fcntl, subprocess, argparse, shutil, datetime as dt, glob\nLAB = os.environ.get(\"LAB\", os.path.expanduser(\"~/projects/siminfeng/campaign/lab\"))\nCID = os.environ.get(\"CAMPAIGN_ID\", \"c1\")\nnow = lambda: dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc).isoformat()\ndef jd(o): return json.dumps(o, sort_keys=True)\ndef sha(p):\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with open(p, \"rb\") as f:\n        for b in iter(lambda: f.read(1 << 20), b\"\"): h.update(b)\n    return h.hexdigest()\ndef sha_s(s): return hashlib.sha256(s.encode()).hexdigest()\ndef sh(cmd, **kw): return subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, **kw)\ndef append(path, rec):\n    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)\n    fd = os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_APPEND | os.O_CREAT, 0o644)\n    try: fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX); os.write(fd, (jd(rec) + \"\\n\").encode()); os.fsync(fd)\n    finally: os.close(fd)\ndef proc_start(pid):\n    try: return open(f\"/proc/{pid}/stat\").read().rsplit(\")\", 1)[1].split()[19]\n    except Exception: return None\ndef last_state(adir):\n    p = os.path.join(adir, \"state.jsonl\")\n    rows = [json.loads(l) for l in open(p)] if os.path.exists(p) else []\n    return rows[-1] if rows else None\n\n# ---- test definitions: how an image's tools are exercised and judged (the \"tests\" half of a lab) ----\ndef load_tests():\n    t = {}\n    import glob as g\n    for f in sorted(g.glob(os.path.join(LAB, \"tests\", \"*.json\"))): t.update(json.load(open(f)))\n    return t\nTESTS = load_tests()\ndef assess(test, out, err, rc):\n    \"\"\"Decode only for parsing; preserve diagnostics and contain parser failures.\"\"\"\n    invalid_utf8 = False\n    try:\n        decoded = []\n        for value in (out, err):\n            if isinstance(value, bytes):\n                try: value = value.decode(\"utf-8\")\n                except UnicodeDecodeError:\n                    invalid_utf8 = True\n                    value = value.decode(\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")\n                # Match the universal newlines of the previous text-mode reads.\n                value = value.replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\").replace(\"\\r\", \"\\n\")\n            decoded.append(value)\n        result = _parse_assessment(test, *decoded, rc)\n    except Exception as exc:\n        result = dict(ok=False, assessment_error=f\"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}\")\n    if invalid_utf8:\n        # Generated text may legitimately end/split inside a multibyte char (byte-level tokens).\n        # Flag always; fail closed only for parsers that extract numbers from the text.\n        result.update(invalid_utf8=True, decode_replacements=sum(v.count(\"\\ufffd\") for v in decoded))\n        if TESTS.get(test, {}).get(\"parser\") != \"completion\": result[\"ok\"] = False\n    return result\n\ndef assess_files(test, out_path, err_path, rc):\n    \"\"\"Read subprocess evidence without modifying it, including on failure.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        with open(out_path, \"rb\") as out, open(err_path, \"rb\") as err:\n            return assess(test, out.read(), err.read(), rc)\n    except Exception as exc:\n        return dict(ok=False, assessment_error=f\"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}\")\n\ndef _parse_assessment(test, out, err, rc):\n    txt = out + \"\\n\" + err\n    if TESTS[test].get(\"parser\") == \"ppl\":\n        m = re.search(r\"Final estimate: PPL = ([\\d.]+) \\+/- ([\\d.]+)\", txt)\n        return dict(ok=rc == 0 and bool(m), ppl=float(m.group(1)) if m else None, ppl_stderr=float(m.group(2)) if m else None)\n    if TESTS[test].get(\"parser\") == \"kl\":\n        g = lambda pat: (lambda m: float(m.group(1)) if m else None)(re.search(pat, txt))\n        return dict(ok=rc == 0 and g(r\"Mean\\s+KLD:\\s+([\\d.]+)\") is not None, kld=g(r\"Mean\\s+KLD:\\s+([\\d.]+)\"), kld_p99=g(r\"99.0%\\s+KLD:\\s+([\\d.]+)\"),\n                    top1_pct=g(r\"Same top p:\\s+([\\d.]+)\"), ppl_ratio=g(r\"Mean PPL\\(Q\\)/PPL\\(base\\)\\s*:\\s+([\\d.]+)\"), ppl=g(r\"Mean PPL\\(Q\\)\\s*:\\s+([\\d.]+)\"))\n    if TESTS[test].get(\"parser\") == \"completion\":\n        m = re.findall(r\"\\beval time =\\s+([\\d.]+) ms /\\s+(\\d+) (?:runs|tokens).*?([\\d.]+) tokens per second\", txt)\n        pe = re.findall(r\"prompt eval time =\\s+([\\d.]+) ms /\\s+(\\d+) tokens.*?([\\d.]+) tokens per second\", txt)\n        dec = [x for x in m]   # lines starting with 'eval time' (decode); 'prompt eval time' captured separately\n        return dict(ok=rc == 0 and bool(dec), decode_tps=float(dec[-1][2]) if dec else None, decode_tokens=int(dec[-1][1]) if dec else None,\n                    prefill_tps=float(pe[-1][2]) if pe else None, prefill_tokens=int(pe[-1][1]) if pe else None, text_head=out.strip()[-160:])\n    if TESTS[test].get(\"parser\") == \"gen\":\n        return dict(ok=rc == 0 and len(out.strip()) > 20, chars=len(out.strip()), head=out.strip()[:120])\n    if TESTS[test].get(\"parser\") == \"bench_json\":\n        try: rows = json.loads(out[out.index(\"[\"):out.rindex(\"]\") + 1])\n        except Exception as exc:\n            return dict(ok=False, parse_error=True, assessment_error=f\"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}\")\n        return dict(ok=rc == 0 and bool(rows), samples=[dict(n_prompt=r[\"n_prompt\"], n_gen=r[\"n_gen\"], avg_ts=r[\"avg_ts\"], samples_ts=r.get(\"samples_ts\")) for r in rows])\n    return dict(ok=rc == 0)\n\ndef gpu_state():\n    q = sh([\"nvidia-smi\",\"--query-gpu=memory.used,clocks.sm,temperature.gpu,power.draw\",\"--format=csv,noheader\"]).stdout.strip()\n    apps = sh([\"nvidia-smi\",\"--query-compute-apps=pid,used_memory\",\"--format=csv,noheader\"]).stdout.strip().splitlines()\n    return dict(gpu=q, compute_apps=apps)\n\ndef read_int(path):\n    try: return int(open(path).read().strip())\n    except Exception: return None\n\ndef cid_cgroup(cname):\n    cid = sh([\"docker\", \"inspect\", \"-f\", \"{{.Id}}\", cname]).stdout.strip()\n    return f\"/sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/docker-{cid}.scope\" if cid else None\n\ndef cmd_image(a):\n    ref = a.ref; r = sh([\"docker\", \"image\", \"inspect\", ref, \"-f\", \"{{.Id}}|{{json .RepoDigests}}|{{.Created}}\"])\n    if r.returncode: sys.exit(\"image not present locally: \" + r.stderr)\n    iid, rd, created = r.stdout.strip().split(\"|\")\n    rec = dict(name=a.name, ref=ref, image_id=iid, repo_digests=json.loads(rd), created=created, registered=now())\n    p = os.path.join(LAB, \"images\", a.name + \".json\"); os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p), exist_ok=True)\n    if os.path.exists(p) and json.load(open(p))[\"image_id\"] != iid: sys.exit(\"name already bound to a different image id (images are immutable)\")\n    json.dump(rec, open(p, \"w\"), indent=1); print(jd(rec))\n\ndef archive_ids(path):\n    \"\"\"Identities read from the docker-save archive itself: config digest (image config) and top-level index digest (what the containerd store reports as Id).\"\"\"\n    import tarfile\n    out = dict(config=None, index=None)\n    with tarfile.open(path) as t:\n        names = t.getnames()\n        if \"manifest.json\" in names:\n            m = json.load(t.extractfile(\"manifest.json\"))\n            if m and m[0].get(\"Config\", \"\").startswith(\"blobs/sha256/\"): out[\"config\"] = \"sha256:\" + m[0][\"Config\"].split(\"/\")[-1]\n        if \"index.json\" in names:\n            ms = json.load(t.extractfile(\"index.json\")).get(\"manifests\", [])\n            out[\"index_entries\"] = [m.get(\"digest\") for m in ms]\n            if len(ms) == 1: out[\"index\"] = ms[0].get(\"digest\")\n    return out\n\ndef cmd_imageload(a):\n    \"\"\"Admit an engine image delivered as a docker archive. Order: (1) archive bytes hash, (2) archive's own config digest vs expected,\n    (3) docker load, (4) loaded image id must equal the archive's index digest (the containerd store's notion of Id).\"\"\"\n    got = sha(a.archive)\n    if got != a.sha256: print(f\"REJECT: archive sha256 mismatch (expected {a.sha256[:12]}..., got {got[:12]}...)\"); sys.exit(10)\n    ids = archive_ids(a.archive)\n    if ids[\"config\"] != a.expect_config_id: print(f\"REJECT: archive config digest {str(ids['config'])[:19]}... != expected {a.expect_config_id[:19]}...\"); sys.exit(12)\n    r = sh([\"docker\", \"load\", \"-i\", a.archive])\n    if r.returncode: print(\"REJECT: docker load failed:\", r.stderr[-300:]); sys.exit(11)\n    cands = ids.get(\"index_entries\") or [ids[\"index\"] or ids[\"config\"]]\n    want = next((c for c in cands if sh([\"docker\", \"image\", \"inspect\", c, \"-f\", \"{{.Id}}\"]).stdout.strip() == c), None)\n    if want is None: print(f\"REJECT: no loaded image has an id among the archive's index entries {[c[:19] for c in cands]}\"); sys.exit(13)\n    a.ref = want; a.op = \"add\"; cmd_image(a); print(\"admitted:\", a.name, \"config\", ids[\"